WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.920
Hugh Jackman's final Wolverine outing, Logan,
arrived to a ton of critical acclaim — but

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.590
in addition to being a great film, it also
ushers in some huge changes for the X-Men

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:11.840
franchise.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.260
From bidding farewell to a few of the franchise's
biggest stars, to showing us essentially how

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.890
the X-Men come to an end, Logan is a game
changer.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:24.390
Here’s why Logan marks the end of an era
for superhero films.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.619
The ‘old’ X-Men movies are over

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:31.359
Since debuting on the silver screen in 2000, the original X-Men cast

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:35.540
has popped up in pretty much every era of
the franchise ever since.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.410
They anchored the original trilogy, returned
for the "future" part of Days of Future Past,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.910
and had cameos everywhere in-between.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:48.180
Now we have Jackman's Logan and Patrick Stewart's
Xavier back to to wrap it all up here.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.720
And that's what this film does — it serves
as a coda for that era of the franchise.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.400
As 20th Century Fox looks to keep the focus
on its younger lineup of stars with the eventual

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:03.020
sequel to X-Men: Apocalypse, this is the bookend
for the stories that started it all.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.620
Jackman and Stewart are ex-X-Men

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:10.049
There's one thing that ties pretty much the
entire X-Men franchise together, regardless

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:12.220
of era, and that's Jackman.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:17.170
Thanks to the fact that Wolverine doesn't
age, and apparently neither does Jackman.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:18.670
Seriously, look at this guy!

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:23.259
Anyway, he's popped up in pretty much every
X-Men film except for Deadpool — and even

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:26.130
in that movie, his face still put in an appearance.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.649
At this point, it's almost not an X-Men movie
without Wolverine.

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:31.250
But all that ends with Logan.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.250
Jackman said:

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.939
"It is my last time.

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:36.259
It just felt like it was the right time to
do it."

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:38.090
So yeah, that’s pretty clear.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.649
While the franchise has found success with
younger actors and new characters like Deadpool,

00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:46.999
losing Jackman means the future of the X-Men
story has never been less clear.

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:51.370
Along with Jackman, the original Professor
Xavier — the guy who started the X-Men in

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.340
the first place — is also calling it quits.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.389
Stewart is one of the most recognizable actors
in association with this franchise, and he

00:01:58.389 --> 00:02:00.170
says he’s retiring, too.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:05.049
James McAvoy has done a solid job of taking
up Stewart's mantle as young Xavier in the

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:10.080
prequel films, but for a certain generation
of fans, Stewart will always be Professor

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.230
X.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.050
It's the final Wolverine solo film

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:19.110
The Wolverine spinoff franchise started out
on a rough note with X-Men Origins: Wolverine,

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.070
which was pretty much a train wreck.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:25.140
But things got on the right path with the
samurai-tinged The Wolverine, which helped

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.320
properly set the stage for Logan.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.320
But Logan marks the end of the Wolverine trilogy.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.420
That's not to say Fox won't eventually recast
the role at some point, but it's still the

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.330
end of an era for Jackman's defining take
on the character.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:39.970
It pushes the limits

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:45.250
The rave reviews all make one thing clear
when it comes to Logan: It's a fantastic movie,

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:49.690
and that isn't being graded on a curve against
the adjusted scale of comic book movies.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.900
Logan is a brutal, beautiful, personal journey
that doesn't try to set world-ending stakes.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:59.080
This is a small story that shows the near-limitless
potential for what can be done within the

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.190
format of a "superhero" movie.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:06.080
Logan is ambitious in its simplicity, weaving
in elements from two-decades of films to tell

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.380
a story about a handful of people just trying
to do what's right.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.110
It’s further proof superhero movies can
just be movies.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.240
And good ones, to boot.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.490
What comes next?

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:21.830
Logan wraps up the old saga of X-Men movies,
but also sets the stage for whatever Fox wants

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:22.830
to do next.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.120
Is it another future-set story after Logan?

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.040
A 1990s-set film following the younger cast?

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.240
A new series of solo films?

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.920
No one knows just yet, but thankfully Logan
also blows the doors open in regards to what

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.490
you can accomplish and the types of stories
you can tell with the X-Men.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:43.540
The possibilities are limitless for Fox to
do some truly creative things with the franchise.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.000
We can't wait to see what comes next.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Thanks for watching!

